The Main model works well with multiple speakers, even when they overlap, and there is background noise. However, where there is much 
background noise that is human chatter, the speaker mapping is not accurate. Thus I have made a new branch in the code which deals with 
background chatter for two people talking in the foreground. So far I have found it works decently for 2 people, but it's not as good as 
the first model without background chatter. I can't guarantee it will work well for more than 2, although you can fiddle with the settings 
and see if this helps.

It's worth noting that sometimes if a speaker coughs or breathes before someone else you have listed speaks first, like in the first few seconds
of the audio especially, the model can allocate the name you put second as the first person to talk of those two. You may want to switch round 
names in this case, whichever model you are using. This can also happen silently in a way that you dont hear but the model does, or at least, 
thinks it does, In which case, it can appear as though the model was wrong and switched round speakers, when it wasn't/didn't, at least according to itslef.
Even when "wrong", things will still stay consistent after this point when it initially decides who is who, proof that the diarisation and 
speaker "blueprinting" is working.
